  "summary": "Held the entire window (57 frames, ~57min). Thesis was intact from frame 0 onward: price moved favorably almost monotonically (0 -> +18 -> dips to single digits/low-20s a few times early -> then a sustained, near-uninterrupted climb to +311 peak around t=46m, settling +240 to +260 by the end). Pullbacks were always shallow relative to the accumulated favorable move (giveback mostly under 20-30% of the local swing, never threatening to erase the gain), and against-fires (KMDR/HA/PROPP) were frequent but never coincided with an actual break of the broader trend -- they read as normal chop inside a strong directional leg, not a reversal. Nothing in the run looked wrong-direction; there was no point where price threatened to round-trip back through entry or even through the early favorable levels. What I'd watch next time: a genuine wrong-direction signal would look like giveback approaching/exceeding 100% of the *cumulative* favorable move (not just the local micro-leg) combined with multiple against-fires clustering AND price making a lower low relative to the entry-referenced trend, not just a local pullback from a recent high."
